Experiment 4: Binary Search with Big-O Analysis
Aim

To search for an element in a sorted array using Binary Search and analyse its time complexity.

Algorithm
Set low = 0 and high = n-1.
Find the middle position: mid = low + (high - low) / 2.
If a[mid] equals the key, the element is found. Stop.
If a[mid] is less than the key, search the right half by setting low = mid + 1.
If a[mid] is greater than the key, search the left half by setting high = mid - 1.
Repeat steps 2 to 5 while low <= high. If the loop ends, the element is not present.
Pseudo code
BinarySearch(A, n, key)
  low = 0, high = n - 1
  while low <= high
      mid = low + (high - low) / 2
      if A[mid] == key
          return mid
      else if A[mid] < key
          low = mid + 1
      else
          high = mid - 1
  return -1
Source code (C)
c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70};
    int n = 7, key = 50;
    int low = 0, high = n - 1, mid, pos = -1, steps = 0;

    while (low <= high) {
        mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        steps++;
        if (a[mid] == key) {
            pos = mid;
            break;
        } else if (a[mid] < key) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    if (pos != -1)
        printf("Element %d found at index %d in %d steps\n", key, pos, steps);
    else
        printf("Element %d not found\n", key);

    return 0;
}
Output
Element 50 found at index 4 in 3 steps
Result

The element 50 was found at index 4 using Binary Search.

Time complexity: Best case O(1); average and worst case O(log n), because the search space is halved at every step.
Space complexity: O(1) for the iterative version.
Binary Search works only on a sorted array.
